This is a bit weird because the way `AsnEncodedData` and `PublicKey` work we don't have a good way to represent no parameters, rather its an empty array.

If all we want to do is fix `EncodeSubjectPublicKeyInfo`, then it would look something like this:

```C#
internal AsnWriter EncodeSubjectPublicKeyInfo()
{
    SubjectPublicKeyInfoAsn spki = new SubjectPublicKeyInfoAsn
    {
        Algorithm = new AlgorithmIdentifierAsn
        {
            Algorithm = _oid.Value ?? string.Empty,

            // Normalize an empty parameter value to null so that the serializer doesn't try to write empty.
            Parameters = EncodedParameters.RawData switch
            {
                [] or null => default(ReadOnlyMemory<byte>?),
                byte[] data => data
            }
        },
        SubjectPublicKey = EncodedKeyValue.RawData,
    };

    AsnWriter writer = new AsnWriter(AsnEncodingRules.DER);
    spki.Encode(writer);
    return writer;
}
```

But we actually have the same bug here:

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/b33f75519ea498df81b38fab75c4813b03c9a3e3/src/libraries/System.Security.Cryptography/src/System/Security/Cryptography/X509Certificates/CertificateRequest.cs#L841

Which can be reproduced with

```C#
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;

using ECDsa ec = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
CertificateRequest rootReq = new CertificateRequest("CN=root", ec, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
rootReq.CertificateExtensions.Add(X509BasicConstraintsExtension.CreateForCertificateAuthority());

using X509Certificate2 root = rootReq.CreateSelfSigned(DateTimeOffset.Now, DateTimeOffset.Now.AddYears(1));

X509SignatureGenerator gen = X509SignatureGenerator.CreateForECDsa(ec);
PublicKey key = new PublicKey(new Oid("1.2.3.4"), new AsnEncodedData([]), new AsnEncodedData([1, 2, 3, 4]));
CertificateRequest req = new(new X500DistinguishedName("CN=test"), key, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);

X509Certificate c = req.Create(
    root.SubjectName,
    gen,
    DateTimeOffset.Now.AddDays(1),
    DateTimeOffset.Now.AddDays(2),
    RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(9));
```


So we have two options here.

1. We can normalize empty arrays to `null`. This is "fine" because empty is not a legal ASN.1 encoding. ASN.1 things can be empty, but they have empty encodings (like an empty SEQUENCE is 0x30, 0x00). This is a truly empty array.

    The downside to this is that any time someone interacts with PublicKey.EncodedParameters, checking for `null` to mean no parameters in the AlgorithmIdentifier is not the right thing to do. We have already missed this in at least two places, likely because there hasn't been a strong use case for no parameters until now.

    The upside is that the changes are relatively simple.

2. We change `PublicKey.EncodedParameters` and its associated constructor input to be nullable, and permit `null`. We have a type system and null, and use real nulls. The certificate PALs would need to be adjusted to return a real null instead of an empty array.

    The downside to this is that is a breaking change and it is more work. The latter I am not really worried about.

    The upside is that we use the type system to actually convey when no values are expected instead of empty array sentinels.

> We change PublicKey.EncodedParameters and its associated constructor input to be nullable, and permit null.

I gave this a quick go and it did point out that we have a number of places where we expose the algorithm parameters, and if we wanted to make them nullable, the API diff would be

```diff
namespace System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;

public sealed partial class PublicKey
{
-    public PublicKey(Oid oid, AsnEncodedData parameters, AsnEncodedData keyValue);
+    public PublicKey(Oid oid, AsnEncodedData? parameters, AsnEncodedData keyValue);

-    public AsnEncodedData EncodedParameters { get; }
+    public AsnEncodedData? EncodedParameters { get; }
}

public partial class X509Certificate
{
-    public virtual byte[] GetKeyAlgorithmParameters();
+    public virtual byte[]? GetKeyAlgorithmParameters();

-    public virtual string GetKeyAlgorithmParametersString();
+    public virtual string? GetKeyAlgorithmParametersString();
}
```

So the scope of the breaking change is bigger than just `PublicKey`, if we decided that "`null` is the way we want to represent no parameters".

That is making me lean more toward using the empty array as we were, and fixing places where we need to check for empty arrays.

That said, using a nullable and a type system did help me uncover more places where we are not handling the empty algorithm identifier parameters correctly, like 

https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/d108be1917222891d3a41ac798441a248a046989/src/libraries/System.Security.Cryptography/src/System/Security/Cryptography/X509Certificates/Pkcs10CertificationRequestInfo.cs#L55

I think using nullable is still the "right" thing to do if it were a do-over, but I am not sure now given the scope of the nullability changes that need to be made.
